    "concept": "Operating Systems - Virtual Memory Objectives & Overhead",
    "markdown": "- **Concept / Theoretical Foundation**:\n  - **Virtual Memory** decouples the logical address space seen by a user process from the physical address space provided by primary RAM using hardware-supported paging or segmentation.\n- **Evaluating Each Statement**:\n  - **Option A (Address Translation)**: Virtual memory hardware (MMU) uses page tables to translate virtual page numbers to physical frame numbers. (**True**)\n  - **Option B (Exceeding Primary Memory)**: By paging inactive pages out to secondary storage (swap/paging space), processes can have virtual address spaces significantly larger than physical RAM. (**True**)\n  - **Option C (Degree of Multiprogramming)**: Because only active working sets of processes need to reside in RAM simultaneously, more processes can reside concurrently in memory, increasing the degree of multiprogramming. (**True**)\n  - **Option D (Context Switching Overhead)**: Virtual memory actually **increases** context switching overhead, because switching between processes requires reloading the page directory base register (e.g., CR3 in x86), invalidating or tagging the Translation Lookaside Buffer (TLB), and managing per-process page tables. It does not reduce context switching cost. (**False**)\n\nTherefore, the false statement is **Option D (Virtual memory reduces the context switching overhead)**.",
